=== L2 Production Processes and Working Practices ===
id: 3337804b-408d-43ca-9d8d-0eecd569b5d8
slug: production-processes-and-working-practices
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:10.96321+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Pre-production, production and post-production: how process and personnel shape the final product.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">From Idea to Audience: The Three Phases</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every media product &mdash; a television drama series, a magazine feature, a music video, a radio documentary &mdash; passes through three broad phases before it reaches an audience. Understanding these phases, the people involved and the technologies used is essential for explaining why media products look, sound and feel the way they do. The spec frames this as the relationship between <dfn class="term" data-def="The planning phase before filming or recording begins, including scripting, casting, budgeting and scheduling.">pre-production</dfn>, production and post-production.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Pre-Production: Planning the Work</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Pre-production is everything that must be decided before a camera rolls or a microphone is switched on. It includes commissioning (deciding which projects to fund and develop), writing and developing the script, casting performers, scouting locations, drawing up a <dfn class="term" data-def="A detailed timeline that sets out when each stage of production will happen and who is responsible for it.">production schedule</dfn> and agreeing a budget. The stronger the pre-production planning, the fewer expensive problems arise during filming or recording.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">In television, a key pre-production role is the <dfn class="term" data-def="A senior creative and managerial figure who oversees a television series across all episodes, responsible for both the writing and the day-to-day production.">showrunner</dfn> &mdash; the person who holds creative responsibility for an entire series. In film, the equivalent executive oversight often comes from a producer or executive producer. These roles shape the product before a single shot is taken, which is why pre-production decisions have such a strong influence on the finished work.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: name two decisions made during pre-production that directly affect what a media product looks or sounds like.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Pre-production includes commissioning, scripting, casting, scheduling and budgeting. These decisions shape the product&rsquo;s genre, tone, form and intended audience before production even begins. Budget limits in pre-production often determine the visual style of the finished product.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Production: Making It</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Production is the phase in which the actual filming, recording or other creative work takes place. A television drama may shoot on location, in a studio or a mixture of both. A radio documentary requires field recording and studio interviews. A magazine requires photography shoots, writing and design. Each media form has its own production conventions, but in all cases the production phase turns the pre-production plan into raw material.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Production teams vary enormously in size depending on the budget and form. A large-scale television production might employ hundreds of crew members; a podcast series might be produced by a single person with a laptop and a microphone. The size of the team, the technology available and the working environment all shape what kinds of product are possible.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Post-Production: Shaping the Final Product</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="The editing, mixing, grading and other technical processes applied to raw footage or recordings after the main production phase.">Post-production</dfn> is where raw footage or recordings are shaped into the finished product. For film and television, post-production includes editing, colour grading, adding visual effects, mixing the soundtrack and creating titles and credits. For music, it includes mixing and mastering. For print media, it includes layout, typesetting and image retouching.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Post-production has been transformed by digital technology. Work that once required large specialist facilities can now be completed on a standard laptop. This has lowered the barrier to entry for independent and lower-budget productions &mdash; and it means that post-production decisions (such as colour grading choices or editing rhythm) are now a significant part of the creative identity of a product, not merely technical finishing.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Outsourcing, the Freelance Economy and the Gig Economy</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Major media organisations do not employ every person who works on their products. Much media production relies on <dfn class="term" data-def="Hiring outside companies or individuals to carry out work rather than doing it in-house.">outsourcing</dfn> &mdash; contracting specialist companies or individual freelancers to provide specific services. A broadcaster might commission an independent production company to make a documentary series; a magazine might hire freelance photographers and writers for specific features.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">This has created a large freelance economy in media. Many crew members, journalists, designers and technicians work on short-term contracts, moving from project to project. This gives organisations flexibility but can create instability for individual workers. Critics of the model, particularly in relation to digital media platforms, describe these conditions as part of a broader &ldquo;gig economy&rdquo; &mdash; one in which workers bear the financial risk of fluctuating demand rather than receiving the security of permanent employment.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in two sentences how outsourcing benefits a broadcaster while potentially creating challenges for media workers.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Outsourcing reduces costs and increases flexibility for media organisations. However, it shifts financial risk onto individual freelance workers, creating a workforce that lacks permanent employment security &mdash; a pattern that has intensified in the digital era.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Platform-First and Streaming-First Production</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">The rise of subscription streaming services has created a distinct working practice sometimes called <dfn class="term" data-def="Commissioning and producing content primarily intended for streaming rather than traditional broadcast or cinema release, often with all episodes released simultaneously.">streaming-first commissioning</dfn>. Rather than making a film or series for theatrical release or broadcast window, a streaming platform commissions content directly for its own service &mdash; and may release all episodes of a series on the same day rather than one per week. This changes the economics of production (budgets and marketing are shaped around subscriber retention, not broadcast ratings) and can also change narrative structure (binge-viewing audiences tolerate different pacing than weekly viewers).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Understanding how the platform a product is made <em>for</em> shapes its working practices and form is a key analytical skill. A radio documentary, a social media vertical video and a prestige streaming drama series are all made through the three-phase process, but the personnel, technologies, schedules and budgets involved &mdash; and therefore the finished products &mdash; look very different.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Every media product passes through pre-production (planning), production (creating) and post-production (editing and finishing) before it reaches an audience; decisions in each phase shape the final product.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Media industries rely heavily on outsourcing and freelance labour, giving organisations commercial flexibility while creating precarious working conditions for many workers.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Streaming-first commissioning has created new working practices in which budget, narrative structure and release strategy are all shaped by the demands of subscription platform economics.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">When a question asks you to <em>explain</em> how production processes affect a media product, avoid simply listing the three phases as a sequence of events. The highest-mark answers connect a specific production decision &mdash; a budget constraint, an outsourcing choice, a streaming-first brief &mdash; to a specific observable quality in the finished product. Show that production conditions cause the content, rather than just describing both separately.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
