=== L3 Convergence and the Cross-Platform Media Landscape ===
id: 4e709171-d479-4b6c-aed2-83588a593de7
slug: convergence-and-the-cross-platform-media-landscape
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:09.548149+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How convergence and cross-platform ownership have reshaped the media landscape audiences navigate.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Convergence?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Twenty years ago, television was watched on a television set, music was purchased on physical media and the internet was accessed via a desktop computer. Today a single smartphone can do all of these things &mdash; and a single global platform might deliver all three simultaneously. This blurring of previously distinct technologies, industries and platforms is what media theorists call <dfn class="term" data-def="The coming together of previously separate technologies, platforms, industries or media forms into integrated systems.">convergence</dfn>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Convergence operates at several levels. At the technological level, different devices can now perform the same functions &mdash; a television set, a tablet, a laptop and a phone can all stream the same film. At the industrial level, companies that once operated in a single sector (say, a telephone network) now operate across content, distribution and platform simultaneously. At the cultural level, audiences engage with content across multiple touchpoints &mdash; watching a series, discussing it on social media, playing a related video game and buying related merchandise, often from the same parent conglomerate.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: name the three types of provider that have converged in the modern media landscape, as described in the specification.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The specification identifies three types of provider whose convergence has reshaped the media landscape: content providers (who make media products), network providers (who transmit them) and platform providers (who deliver them to audiences). Where these were once separate industries, they are now frequently integrated within a single organisation.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Henry Jenkins and Convergence Culture</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The theorist <dfn class="term" data-def="A media scholar who argued that convergence is as much a cultural shift in how audiences engage with content as it is a technological change in how content is delivered.">Henry Jenkins</dfn> offered one of the most influential frameworks for understanding convergence. Jenkins argued that convergence is not simply a technological shift &mdash; it is a cultural one. He described a world in which &ldquo;old and new media collide&rdquo; and in which audiences move actively across platforms rather than sitting passively in front of a single screen.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">A key concept from Jenkins is <dfn class="term" data-def="Storytelling that unfolds across multiple platforms, with each medium making a distinct contribution to the overall narrative world.">transmedia storytelling</dfn> &mdash; a practice in which a single narrative universe extends across films, television series, video games, comic books, social media and other platforms. Each platform adds something unique; the full story only becomes visible to an audience member who engages across multiple forms. This is now a standard industrial strategy for major media franchises, not merely a creative experiment.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Cross-Media Ownership and the Vertical Stack</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="When a single company owns media organisations across different forms and platforms, such as a conglomerate controlling television, newspapers, film studios and streaming services.">Cross-media ownership</dfn> describes the situation in which a single organisation controls media products across multiple forms simultaneously. This differs from simple horizontal integration (owning several of the same type of company) because cross-media ownership spans forms &mdash; the same owner might control a newspaper, a television channel, a streaming platform and a social media property.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">At its most complete, cross-media ownership creates what analysts describe as a &ldquo;vertical stack&rdquo;: an organisation that creates content, owns the network that transmits it and controls the platform through which audiences access it. This gives the organisation extraordinary leverage &mdash; it can favour its own content, control the terms on which competitors access the platform and collect audience data at every stage of the relationship.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">The Second Screen and Platform Integration</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Convergence is not only an industrial phenomenon &mdash; it shapes how audiences actually behave. Research consistently shows that many viewers watch television while simultaneously using a smartphone or tablet to discuss, search or react to what they are watching. This is sometimes called <dfn class="term" data-def="The use of a smartphone, tablet or laptop alongside a primary television screen, typically for related social media activity, searching or interaction.">second-screen viewing</dfn>. Media organisations have responded to this behaviour by designing content and marketing campaigns that actively invite second-screen participation &mdash; using hashtags, live polls and social media tie-ins to extend the audience&rsquo;s engagement beyond the primary screen.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain in two sentences what Jenkins meant when he said convergence is a cultural shift as well as a technological one.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Jenkins argued that convergence changes not only the technology through which audiences receive media but also the cultural practices through which they engage with it. Audiences become active participants, moving across platforms and generating their own responses &mdash; rather than passively receiving a fixed product on a single device.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Globalisation and the Streaming Wars</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Convergence has accelerated the globalisation of the media landscape. A subscription streaming service based in one country can reach subscribers in over 150 countries simultaneously, bypassing the national distribution infrastructure that once made global media expansion slow and expensive. This has created intense competition among major platform providers &mdash; sometimes described as the &ldquo;streaming wars&rdquo; &mdash; in which organisations compete for subscriber numbers, exclusive content and talent.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The consequences for smaller national media industries are significant. When global platforms dominate the market in a given country, they can overshadow local broadcasters and independent producers. They also shape what kinds of content are commissioned &mdash; content with broad international appeal is privileged over regionally specific stories that might have strong local relevance but limited global market value. This tension between convergence-driven globalisation and national or regional media identity is a recurring theme in the Media Industries framework.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Convergence describes the merging of previously separate technologies, platforms and industries; the specification distinguishes content providers, network providers and platform providers whose convergence has reshaped the media landscape.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Henry Jenkins argued that convergence is a cultural shift as much as a technological one: audiences move actively across platforms through transmedia storytelling rather than passively receiving a single product.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Cross-media ownership and global streaming platforms have intensified convergence at the industrial level, creating tension between globally dominant platforms and locally specific national or regional media.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">When applying Jenkins&rsquo; convergence theory in an extended response, do not simply say &ldquo;convergence means everything is on one device.&rdquo; The strongest answers distinguish between technological convergence (devices and platforms merging) and cultural convergence (audiences engaging across platforms as active participants), then show how a specific media product or industry illustrates both dimensions. Examiners reward the distinction between the two levels of convergence.</p>

